## Installation

>[!NOTE]
>Pulling the chart requires access to Istari Digital's Artifactory.
>Please contact the [Support Team](mailto:support@istaridigital.com) for more information.

Instructions for installing the istari-platform chart are available in the IT Admins section of the [official Istari Documentation](https://docs.istaridigital.com/).

`common.mainFqdn` is **required** — set it to the platform's frontend FQDN. The chart derives every platform host and URL from it and fails to render without it.

{{ template "chart.sourcesSection" . }}

{{ template "chart.requirementsSection" . }}

> [!NOTE]
> The `nats` dependency is **required** and enabled by default (`nats.enabled`, default `true`). Deploying it takes a StorageClass for its JetStream volume plus two Kubernetes Secrets you create yourself — read the `nats` block under [Values](#values) before installing or upgrading.
>
> The `jaeger` and `dgraph-sec` dependencies are **optional** (`jaeger.enabled` / `dgraph-sec.enabled`, default `false`). Disabled subcharts are not rendered at install time. See the corresponding blocks under [Values](#values).

## API Gateway

The API Gateway (`apiGateway.enabled`, default `false`) is a reverse proxy that lets clients reach the platform's API services — currently the registry service (the `fileservice` block in values) and the identity service — through **one external API host** instead of one DNS name per service. It routes by path prefix (`/registry`, `/identity`), stripping the prefix before forwarding, and answers 404 for unrecognized paths.

`common.mainFqdn` is **required**, so the chart always knows the platform's API host — `api.<mainFqdn>`, or `common.apiFqdnOverride` for a host that isn't a subdomain of `mainFqdn`. Enabling the API Gateway (`apiGateway.enabled`) deploys the bundled reverse proxy for that host **and** points the chart's services at it: the registry service and MCP (when `mcp.enabled`) receive `ISTARI_DIGITAL_API_URL`, the frontend receives `VITE_ISTARI_DIGITAL_API_URL`, and the browser reaches the registry service through `<your-api-host>/registry`. With the gateway disabled (the default), the chart deploys no proxy and emits none of these variables; the services address each other directly in-cluster. Point each client tool's base URL (the `ISTARI_DIGITAL_API_URL` setting) at `https://<your-api-host>` with no path; clients append the prefixes (`/registry`, `/identity`) themselves.

MCP consumes the gateway as a client; the gateway does not serve MCP requests. No `/mcp` route exists, so `https://<your-api-host>/mcp` returning 404 is expected, not a misconfiguration.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Routing through the gateway and switching authentication to the identity service are **two independent settings**.
>
> Setting `ISTARI_DIGITAL_API_URL` routes a client's registry traffic through `<your-api-host>/registry`. It does **not** switch authentication to the identity service. That is governed by a second value, `identity.clientIntegration.enabled`, off by default and independent of `identity.enabled`: `enabled` deploys the identity-service; `clientIntegration.enabled` moves the registry service, the frontend, and MCP onto it as OIDC clients.
>
> `identity.clientIntegration.enabled` takes effect only with the API Gateway enabled. The chart emits the identity toggle to the registry service, the frontend, and MCP only alongside `ISTARI_DIGITAL_API_URL`, which it sets only when `apiGateway.enabled` — so with the gateway off, `clientIntegration.enabled` is a no-op (the toggle is never emitted without the gateway URL the services need). Enable the API Gateway to move clients onto the identity service.
>
> The former `…_IDENTITY_ROUTER_ENABLED` names (and the frontend's `VITE_IDENTITY_ROUTER_*`) remain supported as deprecated aliases, and are ignored when the corresponding new names are set. Prefer the new names; the aliases will be removed in a future release.

Enabling the API Gateway pulls one additional image, `istaridigital.jfrog.io/customer-docker/istaridigital.com/caddy-fips` (the `istaridigital.com` path segment is part of the repository path, not a typo). Air-gapped installations must add it to their image mirror alongside the chart's other images; it uses the same pull credentials.

Routes for the platform's services are managed by the chart and always present, with no configuration needed. Additional prefixes for in-cluster services the chart does not deploy can be attached via `apiGateway.extraRoutes`. Enabling the API Gateway is one line in your values file:

```yaml
apiGateway:
  enabled: true
```

Expose it the same way as the registry service: point your external load balancer or existing ingress controller at the API Gateway's `kind: Service` on port 80, terminating TLS there (the API Gateway itself serves plain HTTP and requires no Istio). The chart can also create a Kubernetes Ingress (`apiGateway.ingress`) or Istio VirtualService (`apiGateway.virtualService`) for you. Optional per-request tracing lights up automatically when the bundled Jaeger is enabled (`jaeger.enabled`); see `apiGateway.tracing.enabled`.

The proxy software inside the API Gateway (currently Caddy) is an internal implementation detail and may change in a future release; such a change will never affect values other than `apiGateway.registry` / `apiGateway.image` / `apiGateway.tag`.

## Provisioner

The provisioner (`provisioner.enabled`, default `false`) is a one-shot Job that generates and publishes platform secrets. Today it registers the platform's services as OAuth/OIDC clients against the identity service and writes their credentials into Kubernetes Secrets that the consuming services mount; it may grow to other secret-generation uses in future.

It is an ordinary release resource, not a Helm hook, so it needs no special install, upgrade, or uninstall steps: `helm uninstall` removes the Job and its ServiceAccount, Role, and RoleBinding along with the rest of the release, and because the generated credential Secrets are owned by that ServiceAccount, Kubernetes garbage-collects them with it. Only the Terraform state Secret and its lock Lease persist — the kubernetes backend owns them, outside Helm's release — so a later reinstall resumes from the existing state. The Job reruns automatically whenever its inputs change — the pod template (image, env, mounts), the `backoffLimit`, `activeDeadlineSeconds`, or `serviceAccountAnnotations` values, or `provisioner.rerunToken` — because its name embeds a hash of all of them, so each change produces a new Job that Helm applies while pruning the previous one. Domain changes such as `common.mainFqdn` flow through the services' ConfigMaps directly and don't require the provisioner to rerun. An input that does not change leaves the completed Job in place; a completed provisioner Job is expected and safe to leave, not a sign that anything is wrong.

The services that consume the generated Secrets mount them directly, so their Pods stay in `ContainerCreating` until the provisioner Job has written the Secrets, then start normally. A plain `helm install` or `helm upgrade` returns before this settles, and the release converges on its own. If you install or upgrade with `--wait` (or deploy through a tool that gates on resource health, such as Argo CD), allow enough timeout for the provisioner Job to finish — pulling its image and running Terraform — before the consuming services can report Ready; the default timeout is usually sufficient, but a slow image pull (for example from an air-gapped mirror) may need more — raise `provisioner.activeDeadlineSeconds` (default 180) alongside Helm's `--timeout`, since the Job fails on its own wall-clock deadline no matter how long Helm waits.

To rerun the provisioner without changing any input — for example to re-apply after an identity-service issue was fixed out of band — set `provisioner.rerunToken` to any new value:

```bash
helm upgrade --reuse-values --set provisioner.rerunToken=$(date +%s) <release> <chart>
```

A changed token is itself an input, so this renames the Job and reruns it. The value is arbitrary; only a change from the previous value matters.

When enabled, the provisioner registers a fixed set of clients — registry, frontend, and MCP. The OIDC redirect URIs are derived from `common.mainFqdn`: the frontend at `https://<mainFqdn>` and MCP at `https://mcp.<mainFqdn>/auth/callback` (override the MCP host with `common.mcpFqdnOverride`). To use additional, or entirely different, redirect URIs, set them on the identity service directly via its environment — `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_FRONTEND_REDIRECT_URIS` / `..._MCP_REDIRECT_URIS` — which the identity service reads in preference to the provisioner-supplied defaults.

Only the values the provisioner actually generates come from its Secrets — the registry's private key, the MCP client secret, the registry public key the identity service reads, and the identity service's own token signing key and token encryption key. The remaining deployment-specific identity config (the API gateway URL each service reads and the redirect allowlists the identity service registers) is chart-derived and rendered into the services' own ConfigMaps, ahead of the provisioner Secret and any user-supplied source in `envFrom`, so user overrides still win. The frontend's and MCP's OIDC authority is not emitted as its own variable: both derive it from the API gateway URL the chart already provides, which is always present when identity is enabled. The fixed OIDC client ids are service-level defaults, not chart-injected. The frontend has nothing generated, so it does not mount a provisioner Secret at all.

The identity service's keys come from their own Secret, `<fullname>-identity-keys`, as `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_SIGNING_KEY_ID`, `..._SIGNING_KEY_B64` and `..._TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY_B64`. The same names set in `identity.secretName` override them. The legacy `..._SIGNING_KEY` and `..._TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY` override both, so an installation that sets them keeps its keys; remove them to move to the generated or new-name keys (set `SIGNING_KEY_ID` and `SIGNING_KEY_B64` together). After changing a key in an existing Secret, run `kubectl rollout restart deployment/<fullname>-identity`. If the provisioner's Terraform state is lost, it generates new keys and everyone signs in again.

The provisioner runs under its own ServiceAccount and Role. The mutating verbs on Secrets (`get`/`update`/`patch`/`delete`) are scoped **by name** to only the Secrets the provisioner owns — the generated credential Secrets and its Terraform state Secret — so it can modify just those. `create` and `list` can't be restricted by `resourceNames`, so both are namespace-wide: `create` because the credential and state Secrets are created at runtime with names that don't yet exist when the verb is checked, and `list` because the Terraform kubernetes backend locates its state by label. Namespace-wide `list` returns full Secret objects, so the provisioner can **read** every Secret in the release's namespace, and `create` lets it add Secrets of any name — though it can still modify only its own. If that scope matters for your environment, install the release in a dedicated namespace.

The generated Secret names derive from `provisioner.fullname` — `<fullnameOverride>-provisioner` when `fullnameOverride` is set, `<release>-provisioner` otherwise. Because `fullnameOverride` defaults to `istari`, a second release in the same namespace collides on these names — as it does on every other resource the chart names — unless you give it a distinct `fullnameOverride`. When the provisioner runs in a separate release from a consumer (`provisioner.external`), the two instead agree on the names by sharing the same `fullnameOverride` — two separate releases in one namespace can't share a release name, so the override is the only way to make their Secret names match.

## Agent registration

`identity.agentRegistration` registers service agents, for example the Secure Connection Service, with the identity service so they can authenticate with `client_credentials`. Each entry in `agents` runs one hook Job on install and upgrade. On a Zitadel configurator install, these values register the Secure Connection Service:

```yaml
identity:
  enabled: true
  extraEnvSecrets:
  - zitadel-identity-service-env
  migrations:
    runAsJob: true
  agentRegistration:
    enabled: true
    extraEnvSecrets:
    - zitadel-identity-service-env
    agents:
    - name: secure-connection
      keyEnv: ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_SCS_AGENT
      usernameEnv: ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_SCS_AGENT_USERNAME
      providerName: zitadel
      providerTenantIdEnv: ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_SCS_AGENT_PROVIDER_TENANT_ID
      displayName: Secure Connection Service Agent
      platformRoles:
      - secure_connector
```

- `identity.migrations.runAsJob` must be `true`; the chart fails to render otherwise.
- The configurator's `zitadel-identity-service-env` secret is listed twice: under `identity.extraEnvSecrets` for identity-service and the Zitadel import, which reads the management key from it, and under `agentRegistration.extraEnvSecrets` for the registration Job, which mounts only `identity.secretName` and its own list.
- `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_SCS_AGENT` holds the agent's base64-encoded public-only credential blob. Add it to the secret `identity.secretName` names. The identity service never receives the private key.
- `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_SCS_AGENT_USERNAME` and `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_SCS_AGENT_PROVIDER_TENANT_ID` come from that configurator secret.
- The entry leaves out `tenantSlug`, so the agent registers in the tenant the Zitadel import (the `identity.idpMigration` hook, on by default for Zitadel deployments) created for its organization, beside the deployment's people.

| Field | Required | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `name` | Yes | Agent name, used in the Job name and the `istari.digital/agent` label: letters, digits, `-` or `_`, beginning and ending with a letter or digit, at most 63 characters. |
| `keyEnv` | Yes | Env var holding the agent's public-only credential blob. |
| `tenantSlug` | Unless `providerName` and `providerTenantIdEnv` are set | Tenant to register in; the Job creates it if missing. Leave it out to use the tenant mapped to `providerName` and `providerTenantIdEnv`, which needs identity-service 2.0.1 or later. |
| `tenantDisplayName` | No | Display name for a tenant the Job creates. Only with `tenantSlug`. |
| `providerName` | With `providerTenantIdEnv` | Upstream identity provider, such as `zitadel`. |
| `providerTenantIdEnv` | With `providerName` | Env var holding the agent's upstream organization id. Maps the tenant to that organization, so the agent's token carries it for Zitadel role lookups. |
| `usernameEnv` | No | Env var holding the agent's upstream user id, binding the agent to that existing identity. |
| `displayName` | No | Agent display name. |
| `platformRoles` | No | Platform roles, such as `[secure_connector]`, to grant the agent (if active) and other active agents in its tenant with the same username. Needs identity-service 2.0.0-pre.18 or later; an earlier image fails the Job on the unknown flag. |

When an env var an entry names is empty or unset, the Job skips that step instead of failing, so the release stays green before the secret exists; an entry with `tenantSlug` still creates its empty tenant. The exception: an entry without `tenantSlug` fails when its `providerTenantIdEnv` value is empty or unset.

The Jobs use the identity service's `nodeSelector`, `affinity`, `tolerations`, security contexts and image pull secrets.

### If registration fails

- **"is already linked to tenant":** the entry sets `tenantSlug` and `providerName`, but the import already mapped that organization to a different tenant. Remove `tenantSlug` and `tenantDisplayName`, or set `tenantSlug` to that tenant's slug, and upgrade again. The failed run leaves an empty tenant under the requested slug, which you can deactivate.
- **No tenant mapped to the organization:** an entry without `tenantSlug` needs one, which the import normally creates. When the import is turned off, set `tenantSlug`.

## Installing with no identity provider

With `identity.oidc.provider: none`, people sign in with local accounts stored in identity-service instead of through an external identity provider. The chart creates the first platform admin during `helm install`. Use it for a new install. An install with local accounts cannot move to an identity provider; switching means reinstalling with one and adding people again.

> The commands below use the default `fullnameOverride`, `istari`. If you set another, replace the `istari` prefix. If you set it empty, use the release name.

### Requirements

- One Helm release must deploy every platform service you run, including the API gateway. A release that deploys a service without the gateway fails to render, and releases that each enable the gateway conflict over its resources.
- `identity.tag` must be 2.4.0 or later. The chart refuses to render with an older tag.

### Values

```yaml
identity:
  enabled: true
  tag: "2.4.0"
  clientIntegration:
    enabled: true
  migrations:
    runAsJob: true
  oidc:
    provider: none
  bootstrap:
    localAdmin:
      email: admin@example.com
apiGateway:
  enabled: true
```

The chart refuses to render with `none` unless each of these is set:

- `identity.clientIntegration.enabled: true`, so registry-service, the frontend and MCP sign people in through identity-service.
- `apiGateway.enabled: true`, so the chart gives those services their identity-service settings.
- `identity.migrations.runAsJob: true`, so the database schema exists before the hook creates the admin.
- `identity.bootstrap.localAdmin.email`, the first platform admin's email.

The admin joins the tenant in `identity.bootstrap.localAdmin.tenant`, default `default`. Leave it at `default`: it must name an existing tenant, and on a new install only `default` exists.

The chart also refuses to render with `none` if any of these is set:

- `identity.oidc.issuer`, `clientId`, `clientAuthMethod`, `scopes` or `defaultTenantId`.
- An `identity.env` entry that sets `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_IDP_PROVIDER`, `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_OIDC_ISSUER`, `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID`, `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_OIDC_PRIVATE_KEY` or `ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET`.
- `identity.idpMigration.enabled: true`, or `identity.idpMigration.to` unless `identity.idpMigration.enabled` is false.

Each error names the key to change.

If the cluster also runs Zitadel and `istari-zitadel-configurator`:

- Leave registry-service's Zitadel settings and Secrets as they are, including its Zitadel domain.
- Remove `zitadel-identity-service-env` from `identity.extraEnvSecrets`, and from `identity.secretName` if it names it. That Secret sets OIDC client credentials, and identity-service and the bootstrap hook do not start while those are set.

### Choosing the first admin's password

Skip this to have the hook generate a temporary password.

1. Create a Secret holding the password in the release's namespace, before `helm install`.
2. Set `identity.bootstrap.localAdmin.passwordSecret.name` to the Secret's name, and `passwordSecret.key` if the key is not `password`.

The password must have at least 15 characters by default (`identity.localAccounts.passwordMinLength`). The admin still changes it at first sign-in. A password Secret set after the admin exists has no effect.

Keep the Secret while `passwordSecret.name` is set. The hook reads it on every install and upgrade, and does not start without it.

### The Secure Connection Service

Skip this if `secureConnection.enabled` is false.

The Secure Connection Service needs a credential that identity-service accepts, registered as an agent. The chart refuses to render with `none` and `secureConnection.enabled: true` until `identity.agentRegistration` declares an agent with the platform role `secure_connector`. Do these steps once, before the `helm install` or `helm upgrade` that enables the service. You need docker, jq, openssl and kubectl.

1. Generate the credential with the identity-service image. Use the same tag as `identity.tag`.

   ```bash
   mkdir creds
   docker run --rm --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" -v "$PWD/creds:/out" \
     --entrypoint /gen-agent-credentials \
     istaridigital.jfrog.io/customer-docker/identity-service:2.4.0 \
     -out /out/credentials.json -agent-id secure-connection-agent
   ```

2. Store the file in a Secret for the service:

   ```bash
   kubectl create secret generic secure-connection-credentials -n <namespace> \
     --from-literal=ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_CLIENT_CREDENTIALS="$(base64 < creds/credentials.json | tr -d '\n')"
   ```

3. Make a copy with the same `clientId` and `keyId` but only the public key, and store it in a second Secret for registration. Registration then never holds the service's private key.

   ```bash
   jq --arg key "$(jq -r .key creds/credentials.json | openssl pkey -pubout)" '.key = $key' \
     creds/credentials.json > creds/credentials-public.json
   kubectl create secret generic secure-connection-registration -n <namespace> \
     --from-literal=ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_CLIENT_CREDENTIALS="$(base64 < creds/credentials-public.json | tr -d '\n')"
   ```

4. Add these values:

   ```yaml
   secureConnection:
     enabled: true
     extraEnvSecrets:
     - secure-connection-credentials
   identity:
     agentRegistration:
       enabled: true
       extraEnvSecrets:
       - secure-connection-registration
       agents:
       - name: secure-connection
         keyEnv: ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_CLIENT_CREDENTIALS
         platformRoles:
         - secure_connector
   ```

   `name` only labels the registration Job; the agent's identity comes from the credential. The agent joins the tenant in `identity.bootstrap.localAdmin.tenant` unless the entry sets `tenantSlug`. [Agent registration](#agent-registration) describes the other fields, except `providerName` and `providerTenantIdEnv`, which the chart refuses with `none`.

5. Delete `creds/`, which holds the private key.

If the service's Secret is missing, its pods do not start. If the registration Secret is missing, the agent is not registered and identity-service refuses the service's token requests.

### Client addresses and the sign-in limit

identity-service limits sign-in attempts per client address, 30 a minute per replica by default (`identity.localAccounts.loginRateLimit`). Unless it can see each person's address, everyone shares that one limit. Set these before installing:

- `apiGateway.trustedProxies` to the CIDR ranges of the ingress controller and load balancer in front of the API gateway.
- `identity.trustedProxies` to the same ranges plus the API gateway pods' addresses. Pod addresses change, so this is usually the pod network's range.

If `identity.trustedProxies` includes the pod network, allow only the API gateway to reach identity-service, for example with a NetworkPolicy. Otherwise any pod can claim to be a proxy and send a false client address.

This applies to traffic that reaches identity-service through the chart's API gateway. If you expose identity-service through `identity.ingress` or `identity.virtualService`, or run it with an Istio sidecar, set `identity.trustedProxies` to the proxies on that path.

### Installing and signing in as the first admin

If you chose the password, skip steps 2 and 4.

1. Run `helm install`. A hook creates the admin as a local platform admin. Unless you chose the password, it writes a temporary password to the Secret `istari-identity-local-admin-password`. If `helm install` fails at the hook, read its log:

   ```bash
   kubectl logs -n <namespace> job/istari-identity-local-admin-bootstrap
   ```

2. Read the password:

   ```bash
   kubectl get secret istari-identity-local-admin-password -n <namespace> \
     -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d
   ```

3. Sign in with the admin's email and this password, and choose a new password when asked. If not used, the temporary password expires after 168 hours (`identity.localAccounts.tempPasswordTtl`).

4. Delete the Secret:

   ```bash
   kubectl delete secret istari-identity-local-admin-password -n <namespace>
   ```

If you deploy with Argo CD or a similar tool, read the password before the next sync. These tools run install hooks on every sync. Each sync recreates the Secret empty and leaves the admin unchanged, so a password nobody read is lost. Issue a new one with `reset-password`, below.

### Recovering a locked-out admin

identity-service disables a password after 100 consecutive failed sign-ins by default (`identity.localAccounts.loginDisableAfter`). If the only admin's password is disabled, lost, or expired before first use, issue a new temporary password:

```bash
kubectl exec -n <namespace> deploy/istari-identity -- /reset-password \
  -email <admin email> \
  -database-url-env ISTARI_DIGITAL_IDENTITY_SERVICE_DATABASE_URL
```

The command prints the new password once. It also re-enables a disabled password and ends the admin's sessions. Access tokens already issued stay valid until they expire, 15 minutes by default.

